sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch09-14> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "When the outer surface of a radiolarian is covered by a layer of nearly equal froth-like vesicles, silica secreted or adsorbed at the surface collects in the grooves between them, producing a spherical shell of rods in a regular hexagonal network; less regular vesicles give less regular meshes, and repeated layers give concentric shells.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Hexagonal lattice shells form as silica collects in grooves between surface vesicles";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter IX, “On Concretions, Spicules, and Spicular Skeletons”; topic “Radiolarian lattice shells”. Scope: spherical lattice-shelled Radiolaria (e.g. Aulonia, Actinomma, Cenosphaera)"
}
